// `main` is the first thing someone arriving from Rust or C types, and in Burxt it is not the entry
// point — the top-level statements are, and the compiler emits `@main` from them. So a function
// called `main` looks like an entry point and is not one.
//
// This fixture exists because the two compilers disagreed about it until v0.0.124: stage-0 accepted
// it (emitting it safely as `bx.main`, so nothing collided — a trap rather than a crash) while
// stage-1 refused it. No fixture declared any reserved runtime name, so the differential test never
// saw the divergence. `exit` and `result` were the same story.
function main() -> Int {
    return 0;
}

print(1);
